# Database Schema Rules

## Model Definitions
- Use SQLAlchemy for all models
- Follow consistent naming conventions
- Include proper relationships
- Define appropriate indexes

## Schema Structure
```python
class EvictionRecord(db.Model):
    __tablename__ = 'eviction_records'
    
    # Primary key
    id = db.Column(db.Integer, primary_key=True)
    
    # Case information
    case_number = db.Column(db.String(50), index=True)
    county = db.Column(db.String(50), index=True)
    state = db.Column(db.String(2), index=True)
    
    # Dates
    filing_date = db.Column(db.Date, index=True)
    judgment_date = db.Column(db.Date, nullable=True)
    writ_date = db.Column(db.Date, nullable=True)
    eviction_date = db.Column(db.Date, nullable=True)
    
    # Timeline metrics
    days_filing_to_judgment = db.Column(db.Integer)
    days_judgment_to_writ = db.Column(db.Integer)
    days_writ_to_eviction = db.Column(db.Integer)
```

## Data Types
- Use appropriate column types
- Set proper constraints
- Include nullable flags
- Define default values

## Indexing Guidelines
- Index frequently queried columns
- Use composite indexes where appropriate
- Consider query patterns
- Monitor index performance

## Relationships
- Define proper foreign keys
- Use appropriate cascade rules
- Include back references
- Handle deletion properly

## Validation Rules
- Implement data validation
- Use check constraints
- Include unique constraints
- Validate date ranges

## Migration Guidelines
- Use Alembic for migrations
- Version all schema changes
- Include rollback procedures
- Test migrations thoroughly

## Table Structure
- Use clear, descriptive table names
- Follow singular naming convention
- Include created_at/updated_at timestamps
- Implement soft deletes where appropriate

## Eviction Tables
```sql
-- Properties
CREATE TABLE property (
    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    address TEXT NOT NULL,
    unit_number TEXT,
    city TEXT NOT NULL,
    state TEXT NOT NULL,
    zip_code TEXT NOT NULL,
    created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
    updated_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
);

-- Tenants
CREATE TABLE tenant (
    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    first_name TEXT NOT NULL,
    last_name TEXT NOT NULL,
    email TEXT,
    phone TEXT,
    created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
    updated_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
);

-- Eviction Cases
CREATE TABLE eviction_case (
    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    property_id INTEGER REFERENCES property(id),
    tenant_id INTEGER REFERENCES tenant(id),
    filing_date DATE NOT NULL,
    judgment_date DATE,
    judgment_amount DECIMAL(10,2),
    writ_date DATE,
    eviction_date DATE,
    status TEXT NOT NULL,
    notes TEXT,
    created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
    updated_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
);
```

## Column Guidelines
- Use appropriate data types
- Set NOT NULL constraints
- Include foreign key constraints
- Add appropriate indexes

## Relationships
- Define clear foreign key relationships
- Use junction tables for many-to-many
- Maintain referential integrity
- Document relationship types

## Data Validation
- Implement check constraints
- Use enum types for status fields
- Validate date ranges
- Enforce monetary value rules

## Performance
- Index frequently queried columns
- Partition large tables
- Use appropriate data types
- Monitor query performance

## Security
- Encrypt sensitive data
- Implement row-level security
- Use parameterized queries
- Follow least privilege principle

## Maintenance
- Regular backup schedule
- Index maintenance plan
- Statistics updates
- Vacuum/analyze strategy 